% ============================================================================
% states-of-matter — each everyday state of matter and its defining property
% (adj-facts-stdlib, CHEMISTRY).
% ============================================================================
% An early-elementary science fact: matter around us comes in three everyday
% STATES, and each one behaves differently when you put it in a container. A
% solid keeps its own shape; a liquid flows to take the shape of whatever holds
% it (but keeps the same amount); a gas spreads out to fill the whole container.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? matter_state(solid, $Property)      % fixed_shape
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `matter_state(state, property)` carrying THAT ROW'S OWN citation,
% with the envelope's locator and trust inherited (RS-5e, #14986), so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% property WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% state not in the table.
%
% The three everyday states, as a truth table:
%
%     state    property                    what it means
%     ------   -------------------------   ---------------------------------
%     solid    fixed_shape                 keeps its own shape and volume
%     liquid   takes_shape_of_container    flows to fit its container
%                                          (the fixed volume is a SEPARATE
%                                          page sentence, carried as the
%                                          row's `cites` — see Provenance)
%     gas      fills_container             spreads to fill the whole container
%
% The property atoms are chosen to match the source's own wording: a solid
% "holds its shape" (fixed_shape); a liquid "will take the shape of its
% container" (takes_shape_of_container); a gas "fills its container"
% (fills_container).
%
% Note that `plasma` — the "fourth state of matter" — is deliberately NOT a row:
% this beginner library covers only the three everyday states a young learner
% sees (ice, water, steam), so a recall must ABSTAIN on plasma rather than
% invent a property for it. That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to trust.
%
% Provenance: EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN span as its `source` (RS-5e, #14986);
% the locator and trust are inherited from the envelope field-by-field. Until
% this change the envelope WAS the solid sentence, so a `liquid` or `gas`
% recall was warranted primarily by a sentence about SOLIDS, and those two
% rows' own sentences reached no answer at all. The envelope is now the page's
% framing sentence, "We call this property of matter the phase of the matter.",
% which names no state.
%
% THE LIQUID ROW IS NOT A PLAIN RELOCATION, and this is the one place this
% header used to overstate its evidence. It quoted the liquid span STITCHED
% with an ellipsis — "A liquid will take the shape of its container ... a
% liquid has a fixed volume" — but on the page those are TWO NON-ADJACENT
% sentences, separated by a third ("In microgravity, a liquid forms a ball
% inside a free surface."). An elided quotation is not verbatim, and shipping
% it as a row `source` would put a citation on a row that cannot be found on
% its own page. So the row ships the CONTIGUOUS sentence as its `source`:
%
%     "A liquid will take the shape of its container with a free surface in a
%      gravitational field."
%
% That sentence supports `takes_shape_of_container` and says nothing about
% volume, so the fixed-volume half rides as the row's own `cites`:
%
%     "Regardless of gravity, a liquid has a fixed volume."
%
% Both were read out of the fetched page rather than retyped, and each occurs
% exactly once on it. (ADJ-A9 requires a `cites` to carry its own `locator` --
% enforced by the adapter's missing-child error on the second STRING, not by
% the comment above it. Counted across this corpus: 209 `cites` in all,
% ~~87 of them inside a `row` block~~ -- SUPERSEDED, see below -- and ZERO bare
% ones anywhere.)
%
% RE-MEASURED 2026-09-16 (#15378): the row-level figure is 86, not 87, and the
% 209 total is correct. Classified by ENCLOSING CONSTRUCT rather than by indent:
%
%     table-level  122 lines   54 files   (4-space)
%     row-level     86 lines   19 files   (8-space)
%     rule-level     1 line     1 file    (7-space)
%     total        209
%
% over 362 fact files matching adj-facts-stdlib/**/*.adj with CHANGELOG.d and
% *.query.adj excluded. The one rule-level `cites` sits inside a top-level
% `rule { }` block in geometry/shape-composition.adj and is neither row- nor
% table-level; a predicate that assumes the 8-space/4-space pair is exhaustive
% sweeps it into the row bucket and returns 87. Two such predicates do exactly
% that (indent > 4, and innermost-construct-is-not-`table`); which one produced
% the original 87 is not recoverable, so neither is named here as the cause.
%
% This header was the EIGHTH copy of that figure, and the sweep that found the
% other seven could not see it: its needle matched phrasings like "row-level
% cites" and "cites lines" while this line says "cites in all" and "inside a
% row block". A security review found it by running the needle the way the
% shipped prose DESCRIBED it rather than the way it was implemented. The
% description promised a claim class; the implementation delivered a word list.
%
% The `liquid` row's `cites` restates the envelope's own locator. That is
% not the RS-5e convention being broken: ADJ-A9 mandates the source/locator
% pair on every corroboration, while the "a row locator means NOT the
% envelope's page" convention governs a row's own `locator` annotation, which
% this table does not use.
%
% WHAT THIS FILE CLAIMS, AND ITS CEILING. Nothing here carries a `quote ... at
% <byte_offset> snapshot "<sha256>"` annotation, which is the repo's
% byte-provenance mechanism. The spans are labelled with the page they were
% read from and nothing more: this table claims no more than `source_labeled`.
%
% The citable artifact is that page at the `locator`, and each state->property
% row was confirmed against it. `trust authoritative` — grc.nasa.gov is a U.S.
% government (.gov) science-education source. Nothing here is asserted from
% memory. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table matter_state {
    columns state, property

    row (solid, fixed_shape) {
        source "A solid holds its shape and the volume of a solid is fixed by the shape of the solid."
    }
    row (liquid, takes_shape_of_container) {
        source "A liquid will take the shape of its container with a free surface in a gravitational field."
        cites "Regardless of gravity, a liquid has a fixed volume." locator "https://www.grc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/airplane/state.html"
    }
    row (gas, fills_container) {
        source "A gas fills its container, taking both the shape and the volume of the container."
    }

    source "We call this property of matter the phase of the matter."
    locator "https://www.grc.nasa.gov/www/k-12/airplane/state.html"
    trust authoritative
}
